#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503730 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e79fca72-ad43-5c9b-94cd-57daec56de76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "b2c15979-d071-5f8a-b3a3-0e1228124d71" :question/math-academy-id "e-18990" :question/problem "The angles $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary angles. If $m \\angle a = 37^\\circ,$ what is the measure of $\\angle b?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/18/18d67b5d6d05c29fdf286406cdb8f1615f623c942c55d414b0655c8ac0ef242b.png)\n\nSince $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to $90^\\circ.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠a+m∠b & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ {37}^{∘}+m∠b & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠b & ={90}^{∘}-{37}^{∘} \\\\ m∠b & ={53}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503653 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6f00614-8fb3-5aed-8730-2118f31ab4d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Complementary Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "0d68be9a-8e75-5378-a88f-92da1a8cda66" :question/math-academy-id "e-18991" :question/problem "![](images/29/29559fd9cbee104e92c719d4c33dd7560d2e2ab8c4f25490659b73f07350debc.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\angle AOC$ is complementary to $\\angle COD.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we have \n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC=x + 24^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nm\\angle COD=3x.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince $\\angle AOC$ is complementary to $\\angle COD,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+{24}^{∘})+3x & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 4x+{24}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={66}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13c03056-9692-573f-bf7e-b938ec3a6529" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Multi-Step Problems Involving Complementary Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "0c3ef1dc-0262-5bed-9980-1672ca0e6f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-18993" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles. If $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD$ and $m \\angle COB = 50^\\circ,$ find $m \\angle AOD.$\n\n![](images/60/60c48e8ce5fd289f6aa7b80cfd5a99db1adf9a0603e6162c1fb5b4dcf1a7e15d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC+m∠COB & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOC+{50}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOC & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD,$ we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BOD = m \\angle AOC=40^\\circ .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOD & =m∠AOC+m∠COB+m∠BOD \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{40}^{∘} \\\\  & ={130}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72e4154b-bb7a-5029-b698-98588634d677" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle Given the Other Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "af3cc0f3-0971-5b26-b9bc-eda04373f225" :question/math-academy-id "e-2620" :question/problem "The angles $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary angles. If $m \\angle a = 37^\\circ$, what is the measure of $\\angle b$?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/18/18d67b5d6d05c29fdf286406cdb8f1615f623c942c55d414b0655c8ac0ef242b.png)\n\nSince $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to $90^\\circ$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠a + m∠b &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n37^{∘} + m∠b &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠b &= 90^{∘} - 37^{∘} \\\\\nm∠b &= 53^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359aa27d-6050-5c52-8c84-1da4daaa0505" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle Given an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "83b57ed2-9933-51be-8e12-bfd95ea36be0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2627" :question/problem "Let $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ be two complementary angles. If $m \\angle B = 2m \\angle A$, find $m \\angle B$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are complementary angles, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle A + m \\angle B = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nm \\angle B = 2m \\angle A\n$$\n\nwe obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A + 2m∠A &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n3m∠A &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠B &= 2m∠A \\\\\n&= 2(30^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e79fca72-ad43-5c9b-94cd-57daec56de76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "384b4abd-c9f1-5ac0-b6a2-7cfec44d45bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503081} {:answer/id #uuid "4e37cb06-ec2b-5ccb-a346-5a95e805e63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503132} {:answer/id #uuid "869d99de-779b-549d-9fd4-dbcfafe38d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503256} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d0420c-9c00-525a-a905-f6f60c928f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503315} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4f5a20-61e5-5392-b52c-eafc8b11417b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869d99de-779b-549d-9fd4-dbcfafe38d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503256} :answer-field/id #uuid "0653b443-1fb1-519e-9b66-36e351cad279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b2d722a-94fe-5e1c-9243-0a6e73122040" :question/math-academy-id "q-357039" :question/problem "If $∠C$ and $∠D$ are complementary angles and $m∠C={51}^{∘}$, find the measure of $∠D.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a910150e8bcfe1d8b4d5e16dd2244a4f5e4da7d6ad965b606293128f8595fa09.png)\n\nSince $∠C$ and $∠D$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${90}^{∘}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C+m∠D & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ {51}^{∘}+m∠D & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={39}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e79fca72-ad43-5c9b-94cd-57daec56de76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82c97ed-52ff-5c50-a778-f1c7a9334d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f82c97ed-52ff-5c50-a778-f1c7a9334d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503492} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bdecb9-1314-5acf-a81c-06cd8741645f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "828e982d-a9a1-585f-8390-ab20f7e96140" :question/math-academy-id "q-357050" :question/problem "If $∠X$ and $∠Y$ are complementary angles and $m∠X={47}^{∘}$, then $m∠Y=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/80/8079f80c1adad20a0844885f06cb56feb8fc55d8172aa154d4d2c484c3b977cc.png)\n\nSince $∠X$ and $∠Y$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${90}^{∘}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠X+m∠Y & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ {47}^{∘}+m∠Y & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Y & ={43}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6f00614-8fb3-5aed-8730-2118f31ab4d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Complementary Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7a2bfef-660f-5e0d-aa98-11357e54fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a2bfef-660f-5e0d-aa98-11357e54fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503486} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a95e5ea-9be9-5a7c-8a81-f28fbc68d1eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8987b97-dcba-5e61-82e1-c9e2d8c85a88" :question/math-academy-id "q-357053" :question/problem "![](images/93/9306c9b7f6dfed945d955c27d12635bf5b1952288d5c833e22737d4c39da8ad2.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n(x+{15}^{∘})+(4x-{5}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+{15}^{∘})+(4x-{5}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 5x+{10}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={80}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6f00614-8fb3-5aed-8730-2118f31ab4d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Complementary Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7cee008-ea9e-560d-8edd-dcb4a4a245dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7cee008-ea9e-560d-8edd-dcb4a4a245dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503488} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b87d76-8f93-5299-8d61-9cdcdaad3258" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db49ef55-d719-58c2-91e1-31736abaa714" :question/math-academy-id "q-357054" :question/problem "![](images/49/490d1cc553867d48b934ad72c42fa69c355d7d8facf0999034fc6ca9e4fa7323.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n{28}^{∘}+(3x+{5}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{∘}+(3x+{5}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{33}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={57}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6f00614-8fb3-5aed-8730-2118f31ab4d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Complementary Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2aa9996-9161-5cec-982f-46b9d4423301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2aa9996-9161-5cec-982f-46b9d4423301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503444} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb68cbb3-1210-57f4-bccd-9a5ec13e8f90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc96f5c1-f42e-58eb-b44f-349743640f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-357055" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3356cf5182c27467cc122249c4212b81934825bfcb96d4f62489d485218a973.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n(2x+{14}^{∘})+(4x-{8}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+{14}^{∘})+(4x-{8}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{6}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={84}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={14}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6f00614-8fb3-5aed-8730-2118f31ab4d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Complementary Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13831e7b-a8e8-5987-b701-8096b743849b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503021} {:answer/id #uuid "1feb171a-2a91-5365-a3af-20df5c774fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503043} {:answer/id #uuid "337bb9c5-b833-5aa0-a6cf-c8f34550daf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503074} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe44935-734c-53e5-85d7-a7ab8c5cb5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503100} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7d1f62-6e9e-5b82-abd3-7d9c48ddaf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1feb171a-2a91-5365-a3af-20df5c774fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503043} :answer-field/id #uuid "c833a1aa-91de-5985-a518-0a798137fa6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63e22990-215d-5b0e-8964-b23a81f16703" :question/math-academy-id "q-5922" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4ff51ad6db174c8bc6284a247f9ac6460de8b1483c211acaf68c5e8559bfaa5.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n{54}^{∘}+(10x+{6}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{54}^{∘}+(10x+{6}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{60}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={3}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
